  "source": {
    "title": "Londonderry Sentinel, 24 May 1967, p. 12 (\"Scoreboard: Coleraine Borough Council\")",
    "publisher": "Londonderry Sentinel (via the British Newspaper Archive)",
    "kind": "news",
    "check": "twelve elected and four defeated; all sixteen printed votes accounted for.",
    "url": "https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0001480/19670524/111/0012"
  },
  "note": "Election of the Coleraine Borough Council, polled Wednesday 17 May 1967; sixteen candidates for twelve seats (Londonderry Sentinel, 24 May 1967, p. 12). Electorate 6,476; total poll 4,648; 66 spoiled papers; 4,582 valid votes; poll 71.7 per cent. Newcomers took the first four places; John White topped the poll. T. K. Caldwell ('father' of the council) was 13th and Rev. J. Wylie 14th. The Belfast Telegraph (18 May 1967) records that Wylie was the only candidate to stand on a party label, Protestant Unionist; no other label was printed.",
  "correctionNote": "Corrected from the page image of the Londonderry Sentinel, 24 May 1967, p. 12 (council-audit, Nov 2026): the defeated outgoing member printed 'xJ. N. Bamber' is recorded as J. N. Balmer (was Bamber), the outgoing councillor returned in 1964 as 'J. N Balmer' (Londonderry Sentinel, 13 May 1964, p. 14); the printed form is kept in Name_As_Printed."
